1. HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language)
    - HCL is the configuration language used by Terraform to define infrastructure as code.
    - Block Syntax
        <BLOCK_TYPE> "<BLOCK_LABEL>" "<BLOCK_LABEL>" {
            argument = value
        }
    - Example: Resource Block
        resource "aws_instance" "web" {
            ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
            instance_type = "t3.micro"

            tags = {
                Name        = "web-server"
                Environment = "production"
            }
        }
    - Block Components
        resource "aws_instance" "web" {}
        resource → Block type
        aws_instance → Resource type (first label)
        web → Resource name (second label)
    
2. Comments
    Single-line Comment
        # This is a comment
        or
        // This is also a comment
    Multi-line Comment
        /*
            Multi-line
            comment
        */

3. Value Types
    Type	    Example
    String	    "hello"
    Number	    42, 3.14
    Boolean	    true, false
    List	    ["a", "b", "c"]
    Map	        { key = "value" }
    Object	    { name = "John", age = 25 }

4. Variables Example
    variable "instance_type" {
        default = "t3.micro"
    }
    Usage:
    resource "aws_instance" "web" {
        instance_type = var.instance_type
    }

5. List Example
    availability_zones = [
        "us-east-1a",
        "us-east-1b",
        "us-east-1c"
    ]

6. Map Example
    tags = {
        Name        = "web-server"
        Environment = "production"
    }

7. Object Example
    settings = {
        name = "app"
        port = 8080
        ssl  = true
    }
    - A key HCL feature is that maps and objects use = instead of : for key-value assignments, unlike JSON. Terraform also allows trailing commas and supports expressions, variables, functions, and references such as:
        instance_type = var.instance_type
        vpc_id        = aws_vpc.main.id
    - This makes HCL more human-readable and easier to maintain than raw JSON for infrastructure definitions.